While a table’s rows are sized, Ladybird measures the contents of a
cell with a length height as if their percentage heights were auto,
then resolves them against the cell’s final height. A scrollable child
counts as zero, and a replaced child resolves against the cell’s
specified height throughout. Chrome 158 and WebKit 26.5 give the same
results in all twelve cases of the new test, run with engine-compare;
Firefox 151 instead resolves against the specified height (0px, 50px)
everywhere, and caps a max-height at it.

In Blink, ComputeMinimumRowBlockSize() in table_layout_utils.cc lays
cells out with an indefinite initial block size and marks them
restricted-block-size, and CalculateReplacedChildPercentageSize() in
length_utils.cc keeps a replaced child on the cell’s specified height.
WebKit’s RenderBox::computePercentageLogicalHeightGeneric() skips a
cell’s specified height as a basis for non-replaced descendants until
the cell is stretched. Gecko’s ReflowInput::InitConstraints() uses the
cell’s specified height as the basis, which matches the Firefox
results above. (This paragraph is from reading their sources.)
